`aplan import` is lossy by design, and says so (ADR-0012). Only non-writing
invocations are exercised here: a writing command would leave task files inside
this fixture directory and pollute its own input on the next run.

`--dry-run` shows the whole result, including what could not be carried, and
writes nothing. The source says `implemented`; it arrives as a `todo` tagged
`kep:implemented`, because a claim is not proof.

```
$ aplan import kep.yaml --dry-run
would import 1 task(s) from kep.yaml
  KEP-3063  todo  [kep:implemented, kep:stage:beta, sig:node]  Graduate the widget API to stable
    -> aplan/tasks/KEP-3063.md
report:
  provenance: KEP-3063 claims `implemented`; imported as `todo` (the claim is not proof). Close it with `aplan close KEP-3063 --evidence commit:COMMIT_SHA` (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `approvers` is not held by the task model (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `creation-date` is not held by the task model (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `metrics` is not held by the task model (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `see-also` is not held by the task model (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `authors` lists more than one; the task model holds a single `owner`, so only the first was kept (kep.yaml)
  placement: imported tasks belong to no cut; where `require_cut_membership` is set, `check` reports APLAN022 until `--cut <milestone>` or a `cross-cutting` tag places them (kep.yaml)
  placement: imported tasks carry no `effort_days`, because there is nothing to derive one from; `check` warns (APLAN009) until you estimate them (kep.yaml)
(dry run: nothing was written)

```

Opting into `done` needs a date, because a `note` must carry the date it was
captured (`APLAN010`) and aplan does not invent provenance. A usage error, not a
half-written plan.

```
$ aplan import kep.yaml --implemented-as done
? 2
aplan: `--implemented-as done` needs `--as-of YYYY-MM-DD`: a `note` must carry the date it was captured (APLAN010), and aplan does not invent dates

```

The date is checked at the boundary, so a bad one cannot reach a task file.

```
$ aplan import kep.yaml --implemented-as done --as-of yesterday
? 2
aplan: `--as-of yesterday` is not an ISO-8601 date (YYYY-MM-DD)

```

An impossible calendar date is caught too, by the same predicate `APLAN010`
judges with: a boundary check that were weaker than the rule would accept the
date, write the file, and leave `check` to fail on it afterwards.

```
$ aplan import kep.yaml --implemented-as done --as-of 2025-02-29
? 2
aplan: `--as-of 2025-02-29` is not an ISO-8601 date (YYYY-MM-DD)

```

A flavor this build cannot read is refused by name.

```
$ aplan import kep.yaml --format backlog
? 2
aplan: unknown --format `backlog` (this build reads: kep, checklist, roadmap-json, spec-kit, openspec)

```

A dry run validates the destination cut too, so a settled one is caught before
anything is promised, let alone written. Placement guards that only ran at write
time would leave freshly written tasks stranded in no cut behind an error saying
nothing had happened.

```
$ aplan import kep.yaml --dry-run --cut M-done
? 2
aplan: `M-done` is a settled cut with status `done` and cannot gain this member. A `done` cut accepts only proved `done` ledger entries; a `dropped` cut cannot be extended. Choose another cut or use the evidence-resolving ledger workflow.

```

An open cut previews the placement instead.

```
$ aplan import kep.yaml --dry-run --cut M-open
would import 1 task(s) from kep.yaml
  KEP-3063  todo  [kep:implemented, kep:stage:beta, sig:node]  Graduate the widget API to stable
    -> aplan/tasks/KEP-3063.md
report:
  provenance: KEP-3063 claims `implemented`; imported as `todo` (the claim is not proof). Close it with `aplan close KEP-3063 --evidence commit:COMMIT_SHA` (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `approvers` is not held by the task model (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `creation-date` is not held by the task model (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `metrics` is not held by the task model (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `see-also` is not held by the task model (kep.yaml)
  dropped: `authors` lists more than one; the task model holds a single `owner`, so only the first was kept (kep.yaml)
  placement: would add KEP-3063 to `M-open` (kep.yaml)
  placement: imported tasks carry no `effort_days`, because there is nothing to derive one from; `check` warns (APLAN009) until you estimate them (kep.yaml)
(dry run: nothing was written)

```

## A markdown checklist

`--format checklist` reads the commonest planning shape there is: `- [ ]` items under
`##` headings. A checklist carries less than a KEP does, so the report says more. Note
what it refuses to invent: a ticked box is a claim, the list's order is not a dependency,
and the ids are generated because the source has none.

```
$ aplan import roadmap.md --format checklist --dry-run
would import 4 task(s) from roadmap.md
  T-001  todo  [phase-0-the-risky-part, checklist:checked]  Spike the protocol client, including reconnect on a dropped link, because that is where the risk is.
    -> aplan/tasks/T-001.md
  T-002  todo  [phase-0-the-risky-part]  Render a frame end to end.
    -> aplan/tasks/T-002.md
  T-003  todo  [phase-0-the-risky-part]  a nested item
    -> aplan/tasks/T-003.md
  T-004  todo  [phase-1-the-product]  Ship it.
    -> aplan/tasks/T-004.md
report:
  provenance: a checklist has no task ids, so 4 were generated as `T-001` onward; rename them before they are referred to elsewhere, or re-run with `--id-prefix` (roadmap.md)
  dropped: 1 nested item(s) became tasks of their own: the model has no subtasks, so the nesting itself is not represented (roadmap.md)
  provenance: 4 title(s) are the item as written (roadmap.md)
  dropped: list order is not a dependency: nothing was written to `blocked_by`, because inferring one from position would be a guess recorded as a fact (roadmap.md)
  provenance: T-001 was ticked in the source; imported as `todo` (a checkbox is a claim, not proof). Close it with `aplan close T-001 --evidence commit:COMMIT_SHA` (roadmap.md)
  placement: imported tasks belong to no cut; where `require_cut_membership` is set, `check` reports APLAN022 until `--cut <milestone>` or a `cross-cutting` tag places them (roadmap.md)
  placement: imported tasks carry no `effort_days`, because there is nothing to derive one from; `check` warns (APLAN009) until you estimate them (roadmap.md)
(dry run: nothing was written)

```

A file with no checkboxes yields no tasks, and saying so beats reporting a successful
import of nothing:

```
$ aplan import prose.md --format checklist --dry-run
? 2
aplan: prose.md: no importable proposal found: this reads `- [ ]` and `- [x]` rows, and the document has none. It has 1 heading(s). List the items you mean as `- [ ]` rows and import that (`docs/importing-proposals.md`, under "A table is not a checklist")

```

`--id-prefix` belongs to the flavor whose source has no ids:

```
$ aplan import kep.yaml --id-prefix X --dry-run
? 2
aplan: `--id-prefix` applies to `--format checklist`, whose source carries no ids, and to `--format spec-kit`/`openspec`, whose ids it qualifies

```

## A hand-maintained status contract

`--format roadmap-json` reads a status-and-dependency projection: phases, and a workstream
whose milestones carry a `depends_on` graph. The graph becomes `blocked_by`, so a reference
to a milestone that does not exist is `APLAN003` and a cycle is `APLAN004`, neither of which
the source's own linter could see. The workstream itself becomes a cut (ADR-0008), so
`APLAN007` then refuses to call it done while a member is not.

```
$ aplan import roadmap.json --format roadmap-json --dry-run
would import 5 task(s) from roadmap.json
  PHASE-00  todo  [roadmap-json:done]  Research
    -> aplan/tasks/PHASE-00.md
  PHASE-13  in_progress  [roadmap-json:in_progress]  Web explorer
    -> aplan/tasks/PHASE-13.md
  RFC-0001-A  todo  [roadmap-json:not_started]  Containment
    -> aplan/tasks/RFC-0001-A.md
  RFC-0001-B  todo  [roadmap-json:not_started]  Restoration
    -> aplan/tasks/RFC-0001-B.md
  RFC-0001-CUT  todo  [milestone]  Remediation
    -> aplan/tasks/RFC-0001-CUT.md
report:
  provenance: status mapping: `not_started` -> `todo` (not started here either); `in_progress` -> `in_progress` (carried across as-is); `blocked` -> `blocked` (carried across as-is); `dropped` -> `dropped` (carried across as-is); `done` -> `todo` (a hand-maintained `done` is a claim, so it arrives `todo` with the claim as a tag); `not_applicable` -> `dropped` (a requirement that turned out not to apply is a decision, so it arrives `dropped`) (roadmap.json)
  dropped: `format_version` is not held by the task model (roadmap.json)
  dropped: `project` is not held by the task model (roadmap.json)
  dropped: `specs` lists 1 record(s) with their own statuses; not imported, because they are reference documents rather than work (roadmap.json)
  dropped: `workstreams[RFC-0001].authority` is not held by the task model (roadmap.json)
  provenance: PHASE-00 claims `done`; imported as `todo` (the claim is what this tool is here to check). Close it with `aplan close PHASE-00 --evidence commit:COMMIT_SHA` (roadmap.json)
  dropped: PHASE-00 names SPEC-0009 in its prose; kept in the body but not converted to `traces`, because the source gives ids rather than paths and a trace has to resolve (roadmap.json)
  provenance: RFC-0001: `artifact` became a `traces` entry; `check` fails (APLAN008) if that file is not there, which is the point of recording it (roadmap.json)
  placement: imported tasks belong to no cut; where `require_cut_membership` is set, `check` reports APLAN022 until `--cut <milestone>` or a `cross-cutting` tag places them (roadmap.json)
  placement: imported tasks carry no `effort_days`, because there is nothing to derive one from; `check` warns (APLAN009) until you estimate them (roadmap.json)
(dry run: nothing was written)

```

Both of these refusals happen before the first file is written, because an import that stops
halfway leaves the author with files to delete as well as a mistake to find. A source that
declares one id twice would have one task overwriting the other:

```
$ aplan import duplicate.json --format roadmap-json
? 2
aplan: the source declares `PHASE-01` twice; ids are identities, so importing both would mean one overwriting the other

```

And a source with no title for a task writes a file `check` would reject on sight:

```
$ aplan import untitled.json --format roadmap-json
? 2
aplan: `PHASE-01` has no title in the source; a task nobody can name is one nobody can find, and `check` would reject the file (APLAN001)

```
